comment_data.body:
The only "rules" 'mini.pairs' supports is [conditioning on cursor's neighborhood](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/4f97a8771a480bcacf1d1d0dbf82e47f682aba2c/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L8-L9). So you can set up to insert pair only if cursor's neighborhood matches some two-character `neigh_pattern` Lua pattern. You can do this either [inside `config`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L95) for built-in characters or [inside `MiniPairs.map()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L132) for manual mapping.

You case is thus can be supported with this setup:
```lua
require('mini.pairs').setup({
  mappings = {
    ['"'] = { neigh_pattern = '[^\\]%W' },
  }
})
```

Here it will insert `""` only if previous character is not a `\` and next character is not an alphanumeric character. See [here](https://www.lua.org/manual/5.1/manual.html#5.4.1) for more special Lua patterns.

